experienced  successive waves of poor harvests and  famine the population was further beaten  down by warfare on a large scale  especially in northwestern Europe due to  the Hundred Years War and civil strife  over large areas of the continent then  seemingly out of nowhere plague attacked  too many it seemed like the judgment of  God was being hurled at mankind about  ten years after the great famine struck  Europe with a vengeance and just as the  battles that led up to the Hundred Years  War were getting underway in Flanders  a devastating plague was emerging far  away in the south of China some accounts  from the 1330s reported massive  population losses in China perhaps as  high as 90 percent of the population in  a few places like the City of Hope a  estimates suggest that by 1350 one China  had lost between 1/2 and 2/3 of its  population to plague rodent fleas  transmitted the sickness from its point  of origin in the mountainous Central  Asian steppes which had regular  commercial interactions with southern  China thanks to the Mongols rodents  especially rats were everywhere in the  cities of southern China not something  most people would have thought much  about but China was an important source  of goods that were exchanged all over a  year Asia and across the Mongol Empire  and the rodents with their fleas made  happy homes in the warehouses and  transfer stations all along the trade  routes of the Silk Road that crossed  Central Asia and that linked East with  West it is highly likely that Mongol  armies first introduced the plague to  the Chinese city of hope a early in the  14th century  hope-a seems to have been the focus of  plague outbreaks for decades from hope-a  the illness might have spread to Fukien  by 1345 and then to Xiang tune young Chi  and Hunan among other places by the  1350s  the plague also reached the mongol  outposts on the shores of the Black Sea  by 1345 in 1346 the Khanate of the  Golden Horde a group of Mongols was  preparing to attack a Genoese trading  post in Crimea this was the fortified  port town of kapha that i mentioned at  the outset of this lecture the Genoese  were primarily engaged in the russian  slave trade which the mongols sometimes  supported and often opposed by the time  of the siege of cafe  the Mongol army had become infected with  the plague and spread it to the Genoese  colony in kapha  how exactly the disease was spread as a  matter of some speculation the story  that has gotten the most traction comes  from a lawyer of Piacenza who supposedly  heard it from some sailors years later  in this account the Mongols catapulted  the bodies of dead plague victims over  the walls of kapha well not wanting  rotting bodies to pollute their city the  Genoese hauled off and dump them into  the sea thus unwittingly infecting  themselves once they departed kapha in  their ships they brought the plague with  them now unfortunately this is the only  account to provide such an explanation  for the transmission of plague to the  Genoese and it's extraordinarily  suspicious that the Mongols might have  catapulted dead bodies over the walls of  cafes and not outside the realm of  possibility but the likelihood of the  infectious bacterium surviving such an  ordeal is very low far more likely is  that rodents carrying infected fleas  infected the rodent population of kapha  and these infected rodents very well may  also have infected the shipboard rats or  possibly boarded the ships themselves  what the chroniclers of the 14th century  didn't realize was that a human host was  not necessary to spread the disease well  how the plague came to be ungenerous I'd  the point once the ships carrying their  deadly car goes left kava for the busy  commercial ports of the Mediterranean  there was no stopping the disease from  spreading  what kind of disease was this it is rare  to hear of anyone dying from the plague  today modern antibiotics keep it under  control in areas where it still exists  but the black death was terrifyingly  deadly for a population that didn't  understand how disease spreads it  actually represents three forms of  plague the three types of plague are  bubonic  mnemonic and septicaemia of these the  bubonic plague was the most common form  as I indicated the bubonic plague is  usually spread by the bite from an  infected rodent flea as rodents die from  the infection  their fleas seek another source of food  animal blood and so they often jump to  other animals and to humans fleas were  quite common during the medieval period  so most people wouldn't have been much  concerned about the occasional flea bite  the incubation period of the bubonic  plague is about six days the period  between infection and the first symptoms  the initial symptom is a blackish  pustule forming over the point of the  flea bite followed by swollen lymph  nodes nearest the bite this is followed  by hemorrhaging under the skin which  produces bruise like purple blotches  called buboes on the victims skin it is  from this word booboo that the bubonic  plague gets his name the sub contain E  as' hemorrhaging affects the nervous  system which produces neurological and  psychological disorder such as delirium  and stupor bubonic plague is the least  toxic of the three types of plague but  it still kills between 50 and 60% of its  victims pneumonic plague is different  from bubonic plague in that it can be  spread from human to human after a 2 or  3 day incubation period the victim  suffers a sharp drop in body temperature  which is followed by severe coughing and  a subsequent discharge of blood in the  spud on this buttom contains the plague  bacteria making for an airborne  transition as in the bubonic plague  symptoms include neurological and  psychological disorders pneumonic plague  is rather rarer than the bubonic plague  but it's still fatal in over 95% of its  victims  the last and rarest  form the septicemia plague is like the  bubonic plague spread by fleas or by  handling the body fluids of plague  victims its distinguishing feature is  its rep initi death occurs within a day  of infection in many cases it's almost  always fatal well prior to the 14th  century the illness had last swept  through Europe in the Year 541 called  Justinian's plague then after Emperor  Justinian it was most active in the  Byzantine Empire and in the port cities  of the eastern Mediterranean now plague  sporadically cropped up in Eastern  Europe until about the 8th century but  from about the 8th century to the 14th  century Europe was plague free the major  diseases in Europe were smallpox and  measles and a few others but this was a  fairly stable disease pool but let's  return now to the Genoese ships that  brought the plague from kapha to western  europe constantinople known today as  Istanbul and Turkey was the capital of  the Byzantine Empire and an important  commercial port this was the first stop  the Genoa ships made on their way home  once infection passed into the busy port  city of Constantinople it was easily  transmitted along the maritime trade  routes to North Africa and the Middle  East and of course to the port towns in  Sicily and Italy it spread rapidly  through the Mediterranean by 1348 there  were outbreaks in Cairo Antioch and much  of southern Italy although the plague  moved quickly along the sea routes it  also travelled overland city rat  populations intermingled with country  rats and shared their infected fleas I  know this sounds funny but the old city  mouse country mouse stories hold some  truth more commonly livestock could  provide a host for fleas and herds were  often driven from town to the  countryside and then to other towns  plague could also travel with merchants  on  they're pack animals and in their goods  for example wool cloth provided a  temporary home for infected fleas the  Black Death came through the passes in  the Alps to Germany across the Rhine  from France and Holland and throughout  Europe France was probably the most  populated country in Western Europe in  the 14th century the plague entered  France via the Rhone River Valley trade  routes and the Black Death arrived in  Paris in 1348 at its peak Paris was  losing about 800 people a day to the  disease the Black Death is best  documented in Britain where it's  believed to arrived on ships from  Gascony it reached London in September  of 1348 like all medieval cities London  suffered from extremely poor sanitation  allowing the plague to ravage the city  for almost two years it killed roughly  40 percent of the city's population  before abating by 1349 the plague had  reached Bergen in Norway carried there  as near as we can tell on a shipment of  wool from London Bergen was a member of  the Hanseatic League and an important  North Sea port with regular trade to the  Baltic ports plague was particularly  severe in Scandinavia possibly because  the cold weather caused pulmonary  complications and facilitated the  pneumonic form of the disease the plague  killed half the population of  Scandinavia from Scandinavia the plague  moved along trade routes out into the  Atlantic  killing 60% of the population of Iceland  and by winter 1350 it reached  scandinavian settlements in Greenland  where it wiped out most of the surviving  population Eastern Europe was struck not  as violently by the plague as was  Western Europe by 1351 when the Black  Death was moving east along the rivers  and through the Baltic the epidemic was  2 and a half years old it is possible  that by then the underlying back  tyria had mutated into a less virulent  strain but geography might have also  played a role Bohemia which is the  modern Czech Republic is ringed by  mountains on its western and northern  borders and was not a major commercial  center the plague hardly affected  Bohemia at all neighboring Hungary  however lay on a plain that the Danube  River ran through and was hit much  harder so the plague circled Europe from  around 1350 to 1350 one finally reaching  southern Russia where it had first  entered Europe four years before exactly  how many people died from the plague is  it coerced through Europe as a matter of  speculation there were no census  population censuses so we don't even  know what the population of Europe was  before the Black Death mortality  estimates range from 25 to almost 60  percent of the Earth's population most  scholars feel a figure of between 40 to  50 percent is the best guess as I  mentioned earlier the plague also struck  hard in the Muslim world it hit  Alexandria in Egypt in 1347 almost  certainly introduced by Italian  merchants from the Black Sea plague  broke out in Cairo by the autumn of 1348  some sources suggest that by 1349  one-third of the total population and  perhaps 40 or 50 percent of the urban  population of Egypt had died the Black  Death ranks as one of the greatest  disasters of the Middle Ages and one  that affected much of the afro-eurasian  world it even touched north america in  the scandinavian settlements of  greenland the disasters of the 14th  century had long-term effects on much of  the world not only was population  reduced in a horrifying way across  Eurasia but the devastation and social  turmoil also resulted in restructuring  of society and economy the plague was a  devastating phenomenon and could not be  minimized  but the associated troubles that bore  down on Europe the famine persistent  warfare and civil strife and disease  resulted in a very different and perhaps  more resilient economy from what had  existed during most of the Middle Ages  but the value of labor to work the land  increased it was during this period that  entrepreneurial merchants who were not  bound to the land grew in influence the  rise of the Craft Guild succeeded in  paving the way for the development of a  capitalist system in which commerce  predominated the old equilibrium of  agricultural society where labor was  cheap and land deer had been turned on  its head in a matter of only a few years  before the Black Death Europe's  agricultural land was overburdened with  the farming technologies of the time  populations could not grow unless more  land was brought under cultivation the  number of people that a household could  support was limited by the amount of  farmland available households working  with five units of land or less could  not support more than about three or  four people while those with more than  about 20 units could support ten people  as we have seen any decrease in  production put enormous stress on the  population and made untenable the feudal  system in which serfs were bound to the  land once the plague wiped out large  percentages of the rural population this  stress was reduced as demand for land  decreased its value dropped  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

